<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Divan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Divan was the imperial council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, chaired by the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. It brought together the viziers, military judges, treasurers and the chancellor to deal with state affairs, legal cases and petitions. It was the central organ of Ottoman government in the classical period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/divan.jpg" alt="Divan" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Baptiste Vanmour · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Baptiste_van_Mour_006.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The sultan stood at the head of the state and commanded the military and administrative apparatus through the grand vizier and the imperial council (<b>Divan</b>), while religious and legal affairs were overseen by the Sheikh ul-Islam (Şeyhülislam), the empire’s chief religious authority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne was nominally elective, and the Boyar <b>Divan</b> weighed against the prince: from the peace of 1428 boyar coalitions, beginning with Radu II's, challenged established rulers, and the rival houses of Dănești and Drăculești fought over the succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1856 the state was divided into three divisions, Northern Travancore (Cottayam), Quilon and Southern Travancore (Padmanabhapuram), each under a <b>Divan</b> Peishkar ranking with a British District Collector; by 1911 there were five, Padmanabhapuram and Devikulam largely Tamil-speaking, the rest Malayalam-speaking.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
